EverQuest Legends has taken a new approach to the series by designing the game from the ground up to appeal to players who enjoy playing their games solo and/or casually. While groups (up to 4 players) and raids (up to 8 players) are available, the entire game can be played, enjoyed, and experienced solo if that's your playstyle.

Their game formula includes introducing modern game features such as a streamlined user interface, upgraded spell and ability management, and countless quality of life enhancements to the magic and nostalgia of classic EverQuest, including the original art style, graphics, zones, spell effects, loot, and music.

To accomplish this, the team generally focused on fun instead of balance and created an experience that allows solo players the ability to experience 100% of the game (on the easiest difficulty).

Grouping is still an option for those who want to and the team has put in higher difficulties to make it more exciting if players want to challenge themselves, solo or group. Players are incentivized to group with personal loot, meaning players won't compete with each other over drops from a mob - everyone has their own loot pool for each mob.

Turning up the difficulty will reward players with a chance at better drops, instead of receiving the base item as loot, players may receive "Merged" items that are +1 to +4 - upgrades to the base version of that item, which can improve the stats and bonuses, and even grant new and additional stats and bonuses to an item.

This is a modern take on loot because it means that players who stick to the baseline difficulty aren't going to miss out on group or raid loot, it may just take them longer which is fair to all.



